  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAlmost the entire forecast area remains socked in with low\nclouds and chilly NNE breeze. As a result, temperatures have\nreally struggled today, even in the far S/SE, with current\nreadings only in the 50s. There remains a chance (20-50%) for\nsome scattered showers/storms late this afternoon and evening,\nbut as alluded to yesterday, the main focus has shifted to the\nS/SE closer to the surface front. There's a non-zero chance for\na strong storm, or two, with small hail per latest SPC Day 1\nOutlook...but think this is limited to Mitchell County, at best.\nSome forecast guidance attempts to clear us out quite a bit\nlate this evening, then brings in a new batch of clouds right\naround dawn when lows are typically made...which makes for a \ntricky low temperature forecast. Will definitely be a chilly\nnight/morning, but we don't completely decouple the winds, and\nthe arrival of more clouds near dawn should preclude any\nsignificant frost concerns.\n\nCloudy and cool conditions persist all day Wednesday and\nThursday, and eventually some areas of light to moderate rain\n(with embedded weak thunderstorms) will work through the area as\nwell. General expectation is for scattered activity to gradually\nincrease from SW to NE throughout the day on Wednesday, with\nperhaps some areas of drizzle to preclude the organized rain. So\nnot a very pleasant day for outdoor activities, factoring in a\nsteady/chilly NE breeze, as well. Appears the greatest coverage\nand intensity of rain will come Wednesday night into Thursday \nAM, before gradually tapering off Thursday afternoon and \nevening. Activity looks to be most stubborn to taper off \nThursday afternoon across areas N of I-80. Latest EPS ensemble \nremains consistent in that the 25-75th percentile is largely in \nthe 0.25-0.75\" range for total rainfall. 90th percentile data, \nalong with other deterministic output, suggests localized \nhigher amounts around 1\" will be possible - particularly with \nany weak convective elements.\n\nSkies will clear out some Thursday night into Friday morning,\nbut steady Nrly flow should preclude widespread frost concerns\nfor one more night. The same can NOT be said for Friday night\ninto Saturday AM. This period looks to be a classic fall-season\nsetup for widespread frost - light winds or weak return flow,\nclear or mostly clear skies, recent rainfall (damp low levels),\nand seasonably strong high pressure. Not surprised to see\nguidance continue to trend colder with lows Sat AM, and the\nofficial blend now takes portions of the area to or below\nfreezing. Suspect this will be most widespread across the\ncentral and western third of the CWA.\n\nForecast is pretty quiet beyond the Sat AM frost. Temperatures\nshould warm nicely during the day Saturday into the 60s amidst\nplentiful sunshine, then further into the upper 60s to 70s on\nSunday. Bottom line, this weekend looks like a nearly picture-\nperfect fall weekend. Fairly quiet weather and continued\nwarming (perhaps back into the lower 80s for some?) is favored\nfor the first half of next week.",
